azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.
>
> [1]: A cursed Amulet of Yendor should weld to your hand when you wield
> it --SpeedEvil
> [why not have any arbitrary cursed metal object weld to your hand when
> wielded? -az]

The problem with that comes when the player is to offer it, but it's
wielded and cursed.  There is little opportunity to find scrolls of
remove curse or holy water on the Astral Plane, if the player has
neither.

> [2]: Travelers' checks for tourists. Won't be stolen by l's. Don't get
> counted with your visible gold. They are generated bearing the name of
> the original tourist and can only be redeemed for goods/services by the
> named tourist (so worthless in bones piles). --azgor

I dunno, while I'm all for more anachronisms in Nethack, this is kind
of limited in scope.  The things that make Nethack cool tend to be
things that have unlooked-for strategic implications, not minor effect
for a very few people.  Plus, bones files were designed with the
expectation that many different players may encounter one, who are
unlikely to have the same name, and it even suggests players keep the
same name on characters as a strategic choice, which is lame.

> [3]: Breaking a potion with force bolt as a wizard should occasionally
> produce "Wizard shot the potion!" as the message. --Grunt

I like this.  Of course.

> [4]: Water elementals should rust iron weapons and armor.
> --Dentarthurdent

Makes sense.  Also gives them importance beyond just being a (foo)
elemental, like air elementals have now.  (This is all assuming they
don't already.  They definitely should, although not as certainly as
rust monsters do it.)

> [5]: Reading a tin should give a message "This tin has no label" or
> something like that. Maybe create some (low) percentage of tins that
> can be identified by reading the label. --avian

I'd say that 50% of tins should have readable labels.  Identifying tins
is a feature that is almost unused in the game other than just opening
them, because it's so difficult to find a duplicate.  In conjunction
with this, I'd suggest that the smell that wafts up from an open can
should be made sometimes more imprecise, so players get only a general
sense of what's in the can.  If the player's killed one of that monster
before (easy to check) he gets a pretty good sense, if he's eaten one
before (would require better record-keeping unfortunately) he'd have a
perfect sense, if he's never seen one before it'd only be a slight
sense.  I'd finally suggest that the imperfect sense still narrow it
down to monster species (like dragon, lizard, poultry, humanoid/human,
etc) to give the player at least something of a sense if the meal might
be instantly fatal or harmful.

> [6]: Apply a (battle-)axe to a box or chest to break it without
> damaging the axe. --Dentarthurdent

Hm, makes sense for boxes but less so for chests, which may be
metal-reinforced.  Boxes and chests need more differentiation anyway.
Of course the other objects in the box should also have a chance for
destruction (and not just potions either), and a wand that gets severed
this way should have appropriate effects.  That would make the slim
chance that a wand of death gets destroyed a major problem though
unless it were made to be less extremely harmful.  (Maybe the walls of
the shattering box shield the player from some of the effects?)

It differentiates the weapons (which need LOTS more differentiation),
could differentiate the boxes, and could introduce another category of
effects for wands, AND isn't too unbalancing.  Potentially very good.

> [7]: When attacking rusting/rotting/corroding monsters, "Your $weapon
> is not affected" shouldn't repeat while you're still wielding the same
> weapon. --Dentarthurdent

Sometimes the reason the weapon isn't effected, however, isn't related
to its material or rustproofing but to luck or its blessed status.

> [8]: Attempting to genocide an ungenocidable monster should ensure that
> monster is hostile when you encounter it. --jmst

Hm, this must be examined for unforeseen side effects, especially
relating to win-essential actions.

> [9]: Inventory management for pets. This could be as simple as #give
> and #take commands or something as complex as a menu driven interface.
> My goal is to be able to take things out of my dog's mouth or to hand
> armor to my pet Archon without having to wait a year for them to drop
> or pick-up the desired items. --azgor

#give and #take is a good idea (and of course it's up to the pet to
decide whether to accept or give up an item), but control beyond that
is not.  Note that pets are not an extension of the player's influence
into the world but a helper with a mind of its own.  It is not proper
that they be made *too* easy to control.

> [10]: (10 contd.) ...but taking something from your pet reduces
> tameness. --azgor

I'd, again, give the pet the opportunity to refuse to give an item up.
Remember that some angels are degenerated with artifacts, this could
turn into a good way to score Sunsword.

> [11]: an artifact "oLS of some kind. --lament

What kind?

> [12]: in the same vain as poly with DSM on goes to corresponding
> dragon, poly with a complete set (helm, body, gloves, boots) of
> thoroughly rusty metal armor turns you into a Rust Monster, Complete
> set of armor enchanted to -3 or below turns you into a Disenchanter (im
> sure more could be added) --Xarann

Naah.  Remember that rusty and enchanted items are not emblematic of
these monsters, but just a result of their digestive systems.  The
thing you wear to turn into a dragon is NOT Dragon Poop Mail.

> [13]: Make it a configurable option, possibility to make any stack of
> items on the ground more than just 1 but less than the amount required
> for"many" show up as "You see few items here", saving you to skip the
> -more- when walking around many slain monsters --Xarann

Not a bad idea, but has the potential to be one of those little UI
things that few people use.

> [14]: Flux Dragon - just like a normal dragon... but resists polymorph
> and breathes as a wand of polymorph. --Calamarain

Hm, I like there being some intrinsics that a dead dragon cannot
provide.  So far, the only way to get polymorph control involves the
ring of that name, and rings are already a relatively less useful item
type as it is.

> [15]: Genociding green slimes should prevent you turning into one when
> slimed. --jmst

How are you going to get slimed, though, if there are no....

I guess if you're already turning into one.  This makes sense, or it
could just produce a funny message once the transformation is
complete....

